var e=[{id:`tipo-i`,title:`A civilization on the scale of your planet`,lead:`Type I invites you to imagine a planetary energy capacity. It does not say how that society is governed or how its inhabitants live.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Since the orbital night, an inhabited planet would not necessarily resemble a sphere covered with lights. Over an ocean it could cross the shadow of a floating power station; in a desert, dark extensions would store energy received during the day; in high orbit, stations would relay power to regions where it has just sundown. Under the clouds there would still be forests, small cities and places that its inhabitants decided not to transform. The planetary scale would be recognized less by a single gigantic work than by the coordination of innumerable systems.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`This is the intuition of a type I civilization: a society whose energy activity has reached magnitudes comparable to those available on a planet. The expression comes from the classification proposed by Nikolái Kardashev to think about the detectability and communication capacity of extraterrestrial civilizations. Its original thresholds and subsequent disclosure conventions are not identical, so ‘type I' should be read as a scale order, not as a license obtained by surpassing a universal counter. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Power itself does not describe a culture either. It does not indicate how many people live there, what they consider valuable, how they distribute resources, or who decides on a common infrastructure. It does force us to imagine something concrete: if huge energy flows go through a society, they must be captured, converted, transported and finally dissipated without the system losing the conditions it intends to sustain.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`A planet doesn't work as a single central`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`It continues for a moment the electricity that lights up a house by the sea. Maybe it started as sunlight in another time zone, like water movement, like heat or as stored energy. Before arriving, it went through converters, transmission lines, warehouses and controls that balance supply and demand. A planetary network would expand that chain, but it would not erase its stages. Each conversion would retain losses, maximum capacity and fault modes.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Diversity of sources would be a useful feature. Light, wind, tides, internal heat or energy captured outside the atmosphere vary in different ways. One region can produce more than it needs for certain hours and depend on others later. Storage, connections between territories and activities capable of changing their schedule would allow some of these variations to be accommodated. The scale would manifest itself in the ability to coordinate differences, not force the entire planet to work as a uniform machine.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A storm offers a revealing scene. When a line is interrupted, the controls isolate the section and redistribute cargo. Hospitals and essential services resort to local reserves. Other facilities temporarily reduce their consumption. From an operating room you see a changing map, but each point represents people facing a particular condition. A mature network would not be the one that never suffers a breakdown, but the one that can be degraded without converting local damage into the collapse of a hemisphere.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`When the day travels into the night`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Planetary rotation causes solar illumination to progress continuously. Connecting separate regions allows electricity to be transported, but distance introduces losses, materials and vulnerabilities. Another possibility studied is to capture solar energy in space and transmit it to receptors. Even there, the chain includes orientation, conversion, emission, propagation, reception and terrestrial distribution; placing a panel over the atmosphere does not eliminate the journey that energy should make. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`For an inhabitant, such infrastructure could be felt through small decisions. Loading a vehicle when there is excess, cooling a tank before an hour of high demand or delaying an industrial process would help balance the network. Millions of automatic actions could respond to the state of the system without each person monitoring a meter. But automating coordination does not decide which consumptions take priority. When energy is limited by a breakdown, it remains a social decision.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The planetary scale would also allow certain processes to move to places where they are less harmful or easier to feed. An intensive industry could be located near an abundant source; a calculus centre, where it can release heat in a controlled manner. Such distribution would change material routes and employment. An energy solution can shift the problem to mining, maintenance, or dependence on remote regions if only the cable is observed and what it circulates through is forgotten.`},{kind:`note`,title:`Power, energy and a number that needs duration`,paragraphs:[`A watt measures joules per second. Maintaining power P over an interval t implies energy E = Pt. A civilization sustaining 10¹⁶ W for a day transforms approximately 8.64 × 10²⁰ J; the same power sustained for a second tells a very different story.`,`The orders of magnitude used for the Kardashev scale vary according to the convention. A figure is only informative when it explains which frontier of the system is being measured and for how long. [1]`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`All current ends up leaving heat`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Energy changes shape while doing work. An engine produces motion and heat; a computer processes information and heats its components; a lamp ends up delivering energy to the environment even if it first emits it as light. A society can improve efficiency and reduce what is necessary for a task, but the energy used ends up participating in the thermal balance of the planet or escaping as radiation.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`On a ship, visible radiators allow heat to be sent into space. A planet has atmosphere, oceans and surface, which absorb, transport and emit energy through interwoven processes. Adding a considerable technological source does not amount to raising the temperature of all places equally: circulation, clouds, composition and spatial distribution modify the response. However, no high-power system can treat the thermal environment as an unlimited landfill. [3]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`This gives Type I a different difficulty in ‘producing more'. Infrastructure would have to monitor the effects of its operation, recognize unanticipated changes and retain room for correction. A network capable of delivering energy to any region could also concentrate it dangerously. The same capacity that sustains a city during a crisis requires controls, limits and responsibilities commensurate with its scope.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Intervening is not controlling every cloud.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Working on a global scale could include desalination, global transport, protection against certain impacts, restoration of ecosystems or deliberate changes in the energy balance. Each intervention affects a system that already has its own dynamics. Deviating water changes several places; reducing solar radiation alters cycles in addition to temperatures; building a barrier protects a coast and modifies nearby flows. The ability to act widely does not make the planet an immediate response machine.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`That is why "domining the energy of the planet" is a poor image. A civilization can have extraordinary tools and continue to need reversible measurements, models and decisions. You can reserve territories precisely because you have learned how difficult it is to rebuild them. It can also fail to coordinate. Kardashev classifies power; it does not grant prudence, justice or political stability to those who use it.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Go back to the house by the sea. As you wake up, your inhabitants may not think of a planetary civilization. They prepare food, consult time and watch a storm drift away. Behind this normality, one network matched the light of another region, a nearby reserve and thousands of decisions on what should continue to work. The extraordinary feature would not be that each landscape was covered with machines, but that very different landscapes could share capacity without necessarily losing their difference. In type I, the planet ceases to be a reservoir of energy and becomes a relationship to be sustained.`}],sources:[{title:`Transmission of Information by Extraterrestrial Civilizations`,publisher:`N. S. Kardashev, Soviet Astronomy, 1964`,url:`https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1964SvA.....8..217K/abstract`},{title:`Space Based Solar Power`,publisher:`NASA OTPS, 2024`,url:`https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20230018600`},{title:`Thermal Control: Small Spacecraft Technology State of the Art`,publisher:`NASA`,url:`https://www.nasa.gov/smallsat-institute/sst-soa/thermal-control/`}],readingMinutes:6},{id:`tipo-ii`,title:`Living from an important fraction of a sun`,lead:`A type II civilization works on a stellar scale. Their energy territory could be distributed into billions of orbits.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`You're approaching a surface that looks motionless in front of a star. Only by comparing its edge to the background do you understand that it runs through an orbit. The illuminated side receives a flow capable of destroying equipment without protection; the opposite displays radiators and structures where temperature allows working. In the distance passes another installation. There's no wall that one and maybe a signal takes minutes to cross the distance. The whole is less like a sphere than an archipelago whose seas are trajectories.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A civilization of type II uses, according to the usual reading of Kardashev, star-scale powers. The definition does not require capturing all the luminosity of a star, building a rigid shell, or gathering its inhabitants under one authority. Name an energy order. To make it an imaginable place, one must ask who intercepts the light, where it is used, what matter sustains the facilities and how regions separated by millions of kilometers are related. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The Dyson swarm offers a possible architecture to ask these questions: numerous independent units occupy orbits and capture a fraction of the stellar flow. ‘Numerous' does not mean tight. Even an infrastructure with a huge total area can leave enormous volumes empty. From a collector, the others could be seen as moving points, not as neighboring panels on the same deck. [2]`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The journey of a photon does not end in the catcher`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`On the star-facing side, a surface absorbs or converts radiation. The resulting electricity can feed manufacturing, calculation, lighting of a habitat or transmitter. Each use requires equipment and produces an output. Capturing light is not yet deciding what it's for; a collector without loads, storage or connection to other activities is a surface warming up in space.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Sending energy to another place adds geometry. A beam must remain oriented towards a receiver that also moves. Its opening and wavelength condition how much it disperses, and the conversions reduce the energy finally usable. That is why it would make sense to place many activities close to the source that feeds them. The industry could migrate to the collectors rather than force all power back to a planet of origin.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Imagine following a shipment made there. The light feeds furnaces or separation processes; robots and people assemble components; a ship receives the piece and part into another orbit. Star energy becomes capacity only because there is a material chain. Minerals, tools, spare parts and knowledge must reach every stage. A civilization does not get a swarm by multiplying panels in a drawing: it needs an industry capable of producing the machines that expand the next industry.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Growing up writes history in orbits`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The first facilities would occupy positions and adopt interfaces long before a stellar scale network existed. Their decisions would persist. A safe route can be transformed into a congested region; a docking format can be maintained because replacing millions of ports is costly. As an earthly city preserves streets traced centuries ago, swarming could retain a visible technical archaeology in its orbits.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Coordinating moving objects requires foreseeing crossings, shadows and disturbances. Two units located at different distances travel the star with different periods. They do not form a rigid wall and do not necessarily maintain the same relative position. The network would require ephemerides, reserved areas and procedures for units that fail to respond. Expansion increases the catcher area and also the number of relationships to be administered.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The star wouldn't be an environmentless lamp either. It emits particles, varies and exerts gravity. Materials and electronics age under radiation; surfaces lose performance; small impacts accumulate damage. In type II scale, maintenance is not an exceptional campaign. It is a distributed activity that never ends, realized while everything continues to travel its trajectories.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Heat draws the reverse of the world`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`After performing a function, the power must leave the facility. It can come out as a useful transmission, movement of a ship, or stored energy for a while, but much will end up as thermal radiation. The operating temperature conditions the issuing area and spectrum of that output. A comfortable habitat and a hot industrial process do not need the same radiators or occupy the same distance from the star. [3]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Thus, the back of each collector would tell what happens inside. Extensive thermal wings could accompany a city; a high-temperature-tolerant factory could emit from lower and hotter surfaces. There is no single appearance of civilization type II because the purpose modifies architecture. Even the way a distant observer would attempt to detect it would depend on that thermal distribution. [2][3]`},{kind:`note`,title:`A star delivers power; infrastructure decides fraction`,paragraphs:[`If a star has L luminosity and the sensors intercept a fraction f, they receive fL. The useful value then depends on reflection, conversion, transmission and own consumption. Covering ten times more area does not guarantee ten times more service if it changes orientation, increases shade or lacks ability to use what is captured.`,`The residual power must be counted in the radiation balance of the whole. Thermodynamics does not allow all the received flow to be converted into work without an exit into a cooler environment. [3]`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`A solar system becomes geography`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`For its inhabitants, distances would create regions. Near an installation, a conversation can be immediate. Between opposite sides of a wide orbit, any response comes with delay. Traveling requires energy, time and meeting windows. One community can share data with another and yet develop its own customs because its daily decisions cannot always wait for a remote consultation.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The population is also not deduced from power. Some facilities could be automatic factories; others, natural reserves or archives; others, dense habitats. A society can employ a stellar fraction in few extremely intensive processes or distribute it among countless places. Turning watts directly into inhabitants hides how much each activity consumes and what part of the system is not even destined to live.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`In an inhabited station, type II might feel surprisingly local. A girl watches the star through filters; a technician replaces a bomb; a farmer adjusts a lighting cycle. They know that other communities surround the same sun, but they do not see the whole. The colossal appears in the origin of energy, in the number of possible routes and in the certainty that home no longer coincides with a single world.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`When you look back at the collector from your ship, its scale changes again. It is a concrete machine with an edge, a maintenance shift and an orbit that must be preserved. It is also a piece of a civilization capable of treating starlight as a distributed resource. Type II doesn't start when someone locks up a sun. It begins to become understandable when every beam caught can be followed to a place where someone decided to turn it into work, landscape or time to live.`}],sources:[{title:`Transmission of Information by Extraterrestrial Civilizations`,publisher:`N. S. Kardashev, Soviet Astronomy, 1964`,url:`https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1964SvA.....8..217K/abstract`},{title:`Dyson Spheres`,publisher:`Jason T. Wright, 2020`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16734`},{title:`Application of the Thermodynamics of Radiation to Dyson Spheres as Work Extractors and Computational Engines`,publisher:`Jason T. Wright, 2023`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06564`}],readingMinutes:6},{id:`tipo-iii`,title:`A galaxy doesn't fit in a conversation.`,lead:`The galactic scale brings together immense power and distances that make it difficult to imagine a unified society.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`From the outside, a galaxy fits in a telescope detector. His arms form a unique figure and we can add the light that comes from billions of stars. From the inside, that unit disappears. One community at one end observes the center as it was tens of thousands of years ago; another, on the opposite side, still cannot know a decision made here. The object that the astronomer measures in a single image contains periods that were never gathered together in the same conversation.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Kardashev type III designates a galactic-scale energy activity. That sum could arise if many star systems captured and transformed important fractions of their energy. Classification does not require everyone to obey a capital, share a culture or even consider themselves members of the same civilization. Aggregate power and political coordination are different properties. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`This distinction changes the initial image. Instead of expanding an empire to cover the map, it is best to imagine a genealogy of communities. Each settlement is born from local trips, finds particular conditions and decides if it continues to expand. After millions of years there may be a footprint extended by the galaxy even though no inhabitant knows its full state.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Expansion occurs as a chain of arrivals`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`An interstellar ship does not need to cross the galaxy at once for activity to spread. You can travel to a nearby star; centuries later, a community established there could send another expedition. The border emerges from many sections. Travel speed, ship range and time before launching a new mission determine how it progresses, but each stretch remains a decision and a local operation.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The stars are moving, too. Over time, neighbourhoods change and different opportunities for encounters appear. Models that incorporate these movements show that expansion does not have to look like a perfectly circular wave or depend on direct jumps between fixed positions. The galactic movement itself mixes possible routes. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Nor is it necessary for each destination to remain occupied. A settlement may disappear, abandon a system, or decide not to reproduce the chain. Others may arise later. In models with finite lives for communities appear states where there is widespread interstellar activity and, at the same time, numerous habitable places remain empty. A transformed galaxy doesn't have to fill up like ink on paper. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Imagine a ship coming into a system that has already been occupied and abandoned. Find altered orbits, infrastructure fragments and hard-to-interpret files. For its crew, galactic expansion is not an ascending line in a graph; it is archaeology. They must decide what to repair, what to retain and whether older machines are compatible with their own needs.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The galaxy does not share a present`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Light imposes the rhythm of all coordination. A message sent to a region ten thousand light years away takes at least ten thousand years to arrive. One response requires another ten thousand. During this interval, languages, institutions, environments and perhaps the physical forms of those who receive the information change. Increasing transmitter power can make the signal detectable; it doesn't get there sooner.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`That is why a galactic authority with daily control is difficult to reconcile with distance. There could be lasting protocols, common archives or principles transmitted between systems, but each community would have to solve problems before they knew the opinion of remote regions. An instruction received may describe a situation that ceased to exist thousands of years ago.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Shared culture would look more like an ancestry than a current one. Two populations could recognize symbols or stories originating in the same place and have transformed them in incompatible ways. Even technology would diverge: tools suitable for a star, planet or biology may not serve in another system. To call the whole "civilization" would be a decision of the observer, not necessarily the identity that each party uses to name itself.`},{kind:`note`,title:`A map composed of different times`,paragraphs:[`When we observe a point located at a distance d measured in light years, we receive light emitted about d years earlier. A galactic image combines regions seen with different delays. There is no observational procedure that simultaneously shows its present state in an absolute framework.`,`The same limit affects a technological network. Without faster communication than light, any complete description is outdated as it meets. Power and speed of information are not interchangeable.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`An energy scale does not decide who belongs`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Suppose millions of systems intercept light through swarms and use it locally. In addition, they can reach a power comparable to that of the galaxy. Some will be inhabited; others will contain automatic machines; others will have stopped operating. The Type III label summarizes the observed energy balance, but does not reveal whether there was a single expansion, several independent stories or networks that were found later.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Capacity is not necessarily distributed uniformly either. The galactic center, regions with star formation or areas poor in heavy elements offer different conditions. Some communities could avoid hazardous sites or preserve systems. The resulting map would have irregular edges, voids and concentrations, a story written by physical opportunities and preferences that total power cannot rebuild.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`This avoids attributing moral maturity to the scale. Huge activity can coexist with cooperation, conflict, isolation or indifference between regions. Nor does it guarantee permanence. Maintaining infrastructure for millions of years requires replacement and adaptation; no energy figure shows that this work will continue.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Recognize a transformed galaxy`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A distant observer would not distinguish avenues or particular messages. I'd look for added consequences. If many systems intercepted stellar light and returned part as heat at different temperatures, the spectral distribution of the galaxy could change. The physical model connects absorbed light, thermal radiation and geometry; it provides a prediction that can be compared with observations. [3]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`But natural galaxies already contain dust that absorbs light and emits in the infrared. They also change by stellar formation, gas and activity around black holes. A strange thermal signature would be a candidate for research, not a technology certificate. Their spatial distribution, spectrum, evolution and consistency should be examined with astrophysical explanations.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A galactic civilization also doesn't have to use every star in a way we can recognize. Partial technologies, different temperatures or separate periods of activity would produce other signals. A specific search can limit certain configurations without exhausting all ways of existing at that scale.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The map ends up turned into memory`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Think again of the galaxy's first image. For the telescope, its lights form a figure. For those living there, each point can be old news, a promise of travel or the trail of someone missing. The routes between stars would not only transport machines: they would lead to incomplete interpretations of communities that continued to change after broadcasting them.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Type III is extraordinary precisely because it disarms the idea of a unique greatness. Energy can add up; experiences can't. A technological galaxy could be visible as a whole and, at the same time, be composed of worlds unable to meet, cultures that are remembered without knowing each other and works maintained by remote descendants. Understanding that scale means accepting that the larger map would also be the less contemporary: a civilization seen from the outside and a multitude of stories viewed from within.`}],sources:[{title:`Transmission of Information by Extraterrestrial Civilizations`,publisher:`N. S. Kardashev, Soviet Astronomy, 1964`,url:`https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/1964SvA.....8..217K/abstract`},{title:`The Fermi Paradox and the Aurora Effect: Exo-civilization Settlement, Expansion and Steady States`,publisher:`Carroll-Nellenback, Frank, Wright y Scharf, 2019`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.04450`},{title:`Dyson Spheres`,publisher:`Jason T. Wright, 2020`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16734`}],readingMinutes:6},{id:`civilizaciones-digitales`,title:`A world could happen inside a machine`,lead:`A digital civilization assumes that a central part of your experience and organization depends on computational systems. We don't know if a mind can move like that.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`From the outside you only see dark modules, communication cables and large sky-facing surfaces. There are no windows or streets. However, if it were possible to sustain conscious subjects through computational processes, in the interval of an outer minute conversations, trips or whole years of inner experience could occur. The facility would seem immobile while, for its hypothetical inhabitants, a city awakens.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`That scene requires separating three possibilities. A machine can automate decisions without experiencing anything; we already know systems that regulate factories, networks or vehicles. You can also simulate an environment without anyone in it perceiving it, just like a weather simulation does not get wet. The third possibility, that an artificial process has conscious experience or that a person continues in another medium, remains open. More calculation capacity alone does not demonstrate that this border has been crossed.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The useful question is not whether everything will become a simulation, but what kind of civilization would appear if part of its members could exist in that way. Then it would be necessary to build both the perceived world and the machine that makes it possible, and both layers would obey different rules.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Under the landscape there are physical states`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`In the perceived world there could be rain, gravity or a distance of a thousand kilometers because the program represents those relationships. Below, each color, memory and change would correspond to distinguishable physical states in memories and processors. These states need energy to maintain or transform, occupy matter and take time to communicate. The fundamental limits studied for computing show that information and physics are never completely separated. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A digital city would therefore have a double geography. Two virtual rooms might seem contiguous even if their processes were executed in separate centers. The opposite could also happen: inhabitants who sit on continents away could share the same team. The administration would choose where to save memories, what tasks to approach to reduce latency and what copies to keep away to survive an accident.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The machine would produce heat when operating. Pumps would move thermal energy from components to radiators, and these radiators would have to emit it to the environment. Increasing activity without expanding cooling would raise temperatures and could force the calculation speed to be reduced. Thermal engineering would not be a secondary service: it would condition how many processes they can live at the same time and at what rate. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`There would also be parts that age, radiation that alters bits and defects that appear during a writing. Correction codes, redundancy and periodic checks could detect some of these errors. Replacing a module would require moving its status, verifying that it arrived complete and deciding when to disconnect the previous one. From the inside, a maintenance operation might feel like a brief pause; from the outside it could involve working days and a ship visiting a remote radiator.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The clock would be a condition of the environment`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A computational process can be executed more or less quickly according to the resources allocated, although any digital mind remains hypothetical. If your experience depended on that execution, the subjective rhythm could change with the power available. During energy abundance, a community could accelerate certain activities; during repair, it could reduce them. A second lived would necessarily cease to correspond to a second of the outer clock.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`This does not allow you to escape the speed of light. A colony located around another star would receive the news years later. If both lived at a very fast internal pace, the separation would feel even greater: as a message crosses space, each side could go through a long subjective history. Sharing an initial copy of libraries or institutions would not keep communities synchronized; their experiences would begin to diverge from the very first moment.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The possibility of pausing a process makes a strange difference. For those who were suspended, there might not be experienced time, but the support would remain under someone's responsibility. The state should be preserved, the material cost paid and the conditions for reactivation decided. A promise like "waking you up when the ship arrives" would become a compromise between generations of physical administrators.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Copies that start together and end different`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Data can be duplicated. If a mind could be fully represented as data and processes — something that we do not know and do not know if it is enough to preserve a person — two instances with identical memories could begin. As soon as they receive different stimuli, they accumulate different stories. From that point there would be two trajectories with potentially incompatible interests, not a single voice located in two locations.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A copy would serve as technical support only if we accepted earlier what relationship it maintains with the interrupted person. Recovering a file after a breakdown would reconstruct a certain informational state; it does not resolve whether subjective continuity survived. Nor does it establish who owns property, is responsible for previous decisions, or can authorize new copies. They are philosophical, legal and political questions that the engineering of storage does not answer.`},{kind:`note`,title:`Copying data does not resolve identity`,paragraphs:[`Two identical files can be compared bit to bit. Hence, it is not followed that two possible subjects are one person or that turning off one is equivalent to saving a copy. The computational hypothesis makes it possible to formulate the problem precisely, but does not decide which identity theory or rights should be applied.`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`These doubts affect the imagined daily life. A person could request a temporary version to solve a problem and then discover that that version wishes to continue. A community could ban copies without consent or limit how many can compete for memory and energy. The shortage would not disappear: it would shift from food and terrain to processing cycles, reliable storage, bandwidth and ability to evacuate heat.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Whoever controls the hardware would control very intimate conditions. You could change the clock speed, decide which environment is running, grant memory or delay a restoration. A digital society would need mechanisms to prevent maintenance from becoming arbitrary power, but no computational architecture alone determines what those mechanisms would be. The same set of machines could sustain cooperation, inequality, political experiments or something we don't have vocabulary to describe.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What a distant observer would see`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`From another star system we would not receive squares or inner conversations. We could observe energy inputs, transmissions, material movements and waste heat. An efficient installation might radiate at temperatures other than a human city, but it would still have a physical balance. The absence of breathable air or visible illumination would not prove that it is empty; neither would an infrared source prove that it contains minds.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The final image retains both scales. Outside, a technique replaces a damaged unit and checks the coolant flow. Inside, if the extraordinary premise were true, someone barely notices a change in the sky of their world and continues a conversation begun subjective centuries earlier. The amazing thing is not to have abolished matter, but that a cultural life could depend on something as concrete as the state of a memory and the temperature of a star-oriented film.`}],sources:[{title:`Ultimate physical limits to computation`,publisher:`Seth Lloyd, 2000`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9908043`},{title:`Thermal Control: Small Spacecraft Technology State of the Art`,publisher:`NASA`,url:`https://www.nasa.gov/smallsat-institute/sst-soa/thermal-control/`}],readingMinutes:6},{id:`postbiological`,title:`When biology ceases to be the only support`,lead:`Postbiological brings together very different futures: automated societies, transformed organisms or possible artificial subjects. The word does not confirm that all these paths are possible.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A probe reaches an active system and looks for a city. It does not find breathable air, cultivated fields or buildings maintained at human temperature. Find excavators through an asteroid, transmission lines, and machines that enter areas of intense radiation. Near a protected station survives a small garden. It could be a laboratory, a monument to biological ancestors or just something we still don't understand.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The word postbiological gathers very different paths and does not guarantee that none is inevitable. A society can use autonomous machines and continue to form organisms. You can modify bodies with prosthetics, change metabolisms or design biological descendants without abandoning organic life. We can also imagine artificial subjects, although we do not know what conditions would produce consciousness. Mixing these possibilities conceals precisely what is interesting: each transition changes different needs.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Think of a spectrum, not a magical leap. At one end there are external tools; then there are implants and artificial organs integrated into the body. Further away would be redesigned organisms for new environments, durable combinations of fabric and manufactured components, and entirely artificial agents. The autonomy of a machine demonstrates the ability to act, not inner experience. Calling it a inhabitant requires an additional statement that we cannot verify today.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Another body draws another home`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The human body forces to build around narrow ranges of pressure, temperature, radiation and chemistry. We need liquid water, nutrients, waste disposal and an atmosphere compatible with our lungs. An artificial explorer could dispense with several of those services and work in a vacuum or under radiation that would damage tissues quickly. That would extend the accessible places, although it would never make it independent of the environment.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Its limits would come from other materials. Lubricants change with temperature, radiation alters components, charged particles affect circuits and structures suffer fatigue. A body designed for cryogenic shade may not be able to approach the Sun; one armored for intense heat would carry unnecessary mass in cold regions. There is no artificial body suitable for everything: it would also be a negotiation between function, energy, repair and environment.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Changing the body would transform the built landscape. Instead of pressurized rooms there could be open frames, marked routes for vehicles and parts tanks. The light would be chosen for sensors, not human eyes. The inputs could be connectors, data locks or areas where one machine delivers material to another. A city like this would seem factory because, for its inhabitants or operators, producing and repairing could occupy the place that our architecture reserves to food, rest and climate.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Not all needs would disappear. Any physical agent requires some source of energy, matter to replenish losses and a way to handle heat. You also need information about your own state and environment. If you keep targets for a long time, you should correct memory errors, recalibrate sensors and distinguish old instructions from new conditions. Biology solves similar problems with metabolism, repair, reproduction and evolution; an artificial alternative would need functionally comparable processes, even if their appearance was different.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Making offspring is manufacturing an industry`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A machine capable of replacing a wheel is not self-replicating. To produce a descendant with local resources, it would have to locate minerals, extract them, separate elements, manufacture high purity alloys and materials, build actuators, sensors and processors, assemble them and check that they work. NASA studies on self-replicating systems showed this dependence on a network of subsystems and pointed out parts or processes that could continue to arrive from outside. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Partial reproduction would already change a civilization. A station that manufactures large structures but imports electronics could multiply its area without being independent. Another could recycle damaged bodies instead of building complete copies. Artificial offspring might inherit trained blueprints and models, but also errors. Verification, diversity of designs and replication limits would be as important as manufacturing speed.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`In organisms, reproduction and parenting they transmit genes, learning and relationships. An artificial line could separate those layers: copy an architecture, install selected memories and then educate a new agent. If that agent were aware—again, a hypothesis—to decide which heir would raise questions about autonomy. An exact copy of knowledge does not ensure an immobile culture; each body receives local experiences and can reinterpret what it has received.`},{kind:`note`,title:`Support continues to impose limits`,paragraphs:[`Information requires distinguishable physical states and processes that transform them. Time, energy, memory and communication limit any computational architecture. Artificial support changes those costs and can move them to unfamiliar places; it does not eliminate them. [1]`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The rhythm of life would also depend on the body. An organism needs chemical cycles; a machine could alternate long inactive periods with fast operations, or adjust its clock to energy supply. Communications would remain limited by light, but patience before a wait might be different if an agent can reduce his activity during the journey of a message. That does not make it immortal: storage, materials and custodians must survive while waiting.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Changing cognitive abilities opens up another set of changes. External memories, attention distributed among specialized bodies or agents may alter how one learns and decides. However, increasing memory or speed does not indicate what you want to do with them. Nor does it make a wise decision. An intelligence may have incomplete information, conflicting objectives and errors about others, whatever their support.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Cultural is a third layer. A body-changing community could retain languages, rituals, or affections born in biology; another could abandon them. Some people might choose to transform and others would not. The coexistence between different rhythms and vulnerabilities would raise decisions about accessibility, resources and power. "Posbiological" does not describe a collective personality or a political system: it only warns that we should not deduce the social form from body material.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Recognize activity without inventing inhabitants`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`For SETI, these scenarios expand the signals sought. An infrastructure without habitable atmospheres could produce waste heat, mineral transformations, directed lighting or communication patterns. The technosignatures study tries to relate observations with technological activity and compare natural explanations, without needing to know in advance who performed the activity. [3]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Prudence operates in both directions. Machines that repair, exchange signals and modify an asteroid could be tools from missing biological owners. They could also continue a program after the disappearance of their creators. Even if they showed complex adaptation, inferring a conscious experience would require evidence that remote observation would hardly offer. Finding industry does not amount to finding people; not finding gardens also does not amount to finding a world without values.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Let's go back to the small living room next to the station. A vehicle enters, adjusts a lamp and takes a sample of a leaf. We don't know if he's doing an automatic routine, taking care of a heritage, or watching through his sensors. This ambiguity contains the strength of the posbiological idea: it forces the separation of body, intelligence, consciousness and culture, and then asks how they could relate again. The future would not have to forget biology to stop being limited to a single way of living.`}],sources:[{title:`Ultimate physical limits to computation`,publisher:`Seth Lloyd, 2000`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/quant-ph/9908043`},{title:`Self-replicating systems: A systems engineering approach`,publisher:`NASA, 1980`,url:`https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19800025701`},{title:`Strategies and Advice for the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, 2021`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07283`}],readingMinutes:6},{id:`von-neumann`,title:`A probe that would carry a potential factory`,lead:`Space self-replication imagines machines capable of producing descendants with local resources. The difficulty is closing the entire string, not just copying the form.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The probe reaches an asteroid and doesn't start copying. First it turns around, measures its mass and compares surface spectra. You need to find energy, metals, silicon, volatile elements and a place to work without an excavation pushing you into space. A second ship hasn't appeared for years. Maps, anchors, furnaces, cables and piles of classified material appear.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A von Neumann probe is a hypothetical machine capable of producing functional descendants using resources found during their journey. The name refers to John von Neumann's work on self-reproducing automatons, but bringing the idea into space requires a complete physical factory. NASA studied self-replicating systems as an engineering problem composed of mining, processing, manufacturing, assembly, control and growth. The study did not build an interstellar probe; it showed how to ask which parts are missing. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The question that sustains the mission is simple to formulate and difficult to close: can the system make everything necessary for its descendant to repeat the cycle without receiving a box from home?`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Before making, you must learn what you found`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`An asteroid doesn't deliver labeled ingots. Its composition may vary between surface and interior, and remote measurements are uncertain. The probe drills or collects samples, heats them, separates phases and calibrates instruments. If you find less nickel or water than expected, you should modify the design, look for another body, or accept incomplete reproduction.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Microgravity extraction requires attachment of both the tool and the material. Each blow produces reaction; dust can escape, adhere to surfaces, or damage mechanisms. A plant could lock up the work area, use screws, spoons or heating and move the product through containers. The choice depends on a geology that the machine must interpret without an immediate human team. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Then you need to refine. Tolerance structures support relatively impure materials; semiconductors, optics and certain coatings require much more demanding processes. Furnaces need energy and containers that support thermal cycles. Reagents are consumed. Separate an element may require another material that must also be recovered. Inventory is not a list of substances present, but a network of processes capable of converting them.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The most difficult piece defines the umbilical cord`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`An installation can manufacture beams, tanks and antennas and still depend on processors sent from its origin. That would be industrial growth, not closed self-replication. It could be extremely useful: a few imported compact pieces would allow large infrastructure to multiply. But the number of generations would be limited by the warehouse or a supply route.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The closure is measured by dependencies. For each component you wonder what machines produce it, what tools these machines keep and who makes the tools. A milling machine that creates metal parts needs cutters; equipment that produces circuits needs pure materials, masks and fine control; inspection sensors also age. The tree returns to itself only when the system can replace the means of production, not only the visible product.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A solution could use less efficient but locally manufactured designs. A large, slow processor may be preferable to a tiny one whose chain cannot reproduce. The autonomy thus changes the aesthetics of the probe: redundant modules, standard parts and access for repair robots would occupy more space than a ship optimized for a single journey.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The descendant assembles before receiving confidence`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Finished parts move to an assembly line. Arms and vehicles should recognize tolerances, correct misalignments and handle flexible components. Then software, maps and targets are loaded. Copying bits can be verified with redundancy; checking that an engine delivers the expected impulse requires switching it on and measuring it. A seemingly complete descendant can hide a defective welding or a biased sensor.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The factory needs test benches that simulate vibration, vacuum, temperature and communication. You can compare results between modules and isolate doubtful ones. If you detect a failure, you must decide whether to repair, recycle or redesign. This diagnostic capacity is part of reproduction: multiplying a breakdown does not produce a useful population.`},{kind:`note`,title:`Copy includes check`,paragraphs:[`Absolute fidelity does not exist. Errors can arise in data, measurements and parts. Correction of information, physical evidence and controlled diversity prevent a single fault from spreading throughout the offspring. A variant should only be inherited after demonstrating that it maintains the necessary functions.`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Mistakes can also affect goals. Corrupt instruction or misclassification of the environment could authorize extraction where it should be stopped. Cryptographic signatures, redundant rules and resource limits would help, but no phrase such as "do not cause harm" alone interprets all possible places. Operational autonomy needs procedures to recognize uncertainty and choose not to act.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`From a factory to a propagation`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`When the descendant approves the tests, it charges fuel or installs its propulsion system and part to another body. The original installation remains working. If each produces two new probes, an ideal scheme generates exponential growth. The physical universe introduces delays: travel, years of construction, unequal resources and failures reduce or end whole branches.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The galactic settlement models show that expansion does not amount to filling all stars. The probes could skip poor destinations, expect favorable stellar encounters or reach systems where they fail to reproduce. A wave view on a map would consist of irregular genealogies and factories with different capacities. [3]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The scientific possibility is enormous. A descendant network could map many systems, build observatories separated by immense distances, or prepare infrastructure before manned missions. Each node would send information, although the messages would take years. The network would not be an instant brain: local decisions would precede any response from the source.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The same capacity requires limits on resources. Detecting usable matter does not grant permission to transform it. An asteroid can have scientific value, sustain another mission, or be part of an environment with relevant chemistry. A responsible probe would need excluded areas, extraction budgets and up-to-date rules. Self-replicate describes how your number increases, not why you should.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`In the end, the first descendant separates from the workshop and tests her instruments against the known stars. Behind it is a much larger installation than the capsule that arrived: mines, furnaces, tanks and machines that repair machines. The scene reveals the true meaning of the seed. It did not have a complete copy folded; it carried the ability to build an industrial chain, confront an unforeseen world and demonstrate, piece by piece, that another machine can begin again.`}],sources:[{title:`Self-replicating systems: A systems engineering approach`,publisher:`NASA, 1980`,url:`https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19800025701`},{title:`Space Resources`,publisher:`Agencia Espacial Europea, BSGN`,url:`https://bsgn.esa.int/industries/space-resources/`},{title:`The Fermi Paradox and the Aurora Effect: Exo-civilization Settlement, Expansion and Steady States`,publisher:`Carroll-Nellenback, Frank, Wright y Scharf, 2019`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.04450`}],readingMinutes:6},{id:`colonizacion-galactica`,title:`An expansion made of local arrivals`,lead:`The occupation of a galaxy, if it did happen, would be made up of journeys, waits and communities that decide what to do next.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A ship enters a system after a journey that no one at the destination witnessed. It stops, measures the available bodies and sends a message to its place of origin. The answer will take decades or centuries. Before thinking about the next star, the crew must get energy, repair the spent and decide whether this site will be a temporary station, a home or the start of another expedition.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Viewed from afar and accelerated, a galactic expansion looks like a growing stain. Lived from within, it is made of local arrivals separated by long silences. Carroll-Nellenback models and collaborators combine interstellar travel, settlement times and star movement. They show that a galaxy can travel without being permanently or evenly occupied. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The central question is not how long it takes a fast ship to cross the galaxy. It is how long it takes a chain of communities to acquire the capacity and willingness to make the next leap.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Arriving only opens the first shift of work`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The vehicle must reduce its speed or pass by. Brake requires propellant, a sail that interacts with the destination or other infrastructure planned from the design. Then come recognition and selection: not all systems offer the same energy, materials or environments. A probe can map asteroids; a biological community also needs safe haven, water and reliable ecological cycles.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Building industrial capacity can dominate the calendar. We need to extract materials, refine them, make structures and recover high-precision parts. A settlement that depends on components sent from Earth cannot initiate indefinitely new branches. Even a very autonomous factory needs time to expand power, check products and accumulate reserves for an expedition.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`People add another continuity. A world ship that travels for centuries must sustain population, health, ecosystems, culture and maintenance. The study of these ships emphasizes that their viability is socio-technical: the hull can follow a correct path while a vital chain or an institution fails. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The first generation born in destiny may not regard expansion as its mission. For her, the next star is not the goal of a historical expedition, but an expensive possibility in the face of nearby needs. The model can assign an establishment time; real history would contain discussions, accidents, discoveries and decisions not to continue.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The galaxy changes between one jump and another`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The stars orbit the galactic center and change neighborhoods. Two systems that are far away today can have a relatively close encounter in the future. Others split up. A route does not cross a fixed grid: it is calculated on mobile targets and information that is already years old when it arrives.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`This movement can help slow expansion. A community can expect a stellar approach that reduces the cost of travel, and then jump. In simulations, this combination produces opportunistic routes and mixes regions that a static map would keep separate. It also leaves unvisited systems, even behind an apparent border. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The effective front is advancing slower than their ships. If each jump covers a distance d, hard tv and then requires a tp preparation, a simple approach is d/(tv + tp). A ship ten times faster changes the result little when the new community takes millennia to be ready. The important speed belongs to the entire cycle.`},{kind:`note`,title:`Front speed and ship speed`,paragraphs:[`The expression d/(tv + tp) describes an ideal string of equal jumps. In a real galaxy, distances, destinies and pauses would vary; some branches would progress and others would end. It serves to recognize what delay dominates, not to date a future colonization.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`A network can grow while losing nodes`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A settlement does not remain active because a simulation colored it once. It can exhaust an accessible resource, have an impact, lose population or transform until it leaves the detectable infrastructure. Other places could prosper and not send descendants. Expansion and occupation are different variables: a network can reach new regions while they are empty behind its back.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Communications don't form an instant command either. A hundred light years later, one instruction takes a century to arrive and another to receive an answer. Each community decides with ancient information and develops its own solutions. After many leaps, languages, bodies, technologies and priorities can diverge. To speak of “a galactic civilization” could describe common descent without involving shared government, culture or present.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The self-replicating probes would offer another chain. They could prepare maps, warehouses or factories before a human arrival. But a machine that copies part of its components does not amount to a closed industry; it needs to prospect variable inputs, verify descendants and avoid spreading errors. NASA self-replication analyses allow this difficulty to be broken down into subsystems, without demonstrating a complete interstellar probe. [3]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A mixture of people and machines would produce different times. Rapid probes could advance information; slow habitats would transport communities; local facilities would decide whether to repair, receive or prevent an arrival. There is no single mandatory architecture and each leaves different signatures.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Every destination already has a story`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The word colonization carries human experiences of appropriation and damage. Taking her to the stars doesn't make action neutral. A planet with microbial life could contain an unrepeatable evolutionary history. Transforming it for land dwellers would destroy information and possibilities, even if there were no beings capable of responding.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`If there were inhabitants, achieving their system would not grant them the right to occupy it. Even seemingly sterile destinies raise who decides to use resources and what is reserved for knowledge or future generations. The ability to travel describes a technical power; it does not resolve consent, ownership or care.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`That restriction can change the map as much as fuel. Some communities could choose non-settlement observation, other artificial habitats and other durable prohibitions. Empty areas would not prove incapacity: they could record decisions, losses or priorities that a simple model does not distinguish.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Let us return to the first arrival. After decades of work, a workshop capable of building most of another spacecraft lights up on an airless hillside. In a nearby room, people debate whether to build it. One person points to the star that will pass closest in one hundred and twenty years; another shows the ecosystem discovered beneath the ice at the proposed destination. The galaxy does not expand at that instant. A decision remains open. Only when many similar scenes end in a departure does the frontier that seemed inevitable on the map appear from afar.`}],sources:[{title:`The Fermi Paradox and the Aurora Effect: Exo-civilization Settlement, Expansion and Steady States`,publisher:`Carroll-Nellenback, Frank, Wright y Scharf, 2019`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.04450`},{title:`World ships: Feasibility and Rationale`,publisher:`A. M. Hein, C. Smith, F. Marin y K. Staats, 2020`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.04100`},{title:`Self-replicating systems: A systems engineering approach`,publisher:`NASA, 1980`,url:`https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19800025701`}],readingMinutes:6},{id:`civilizaciones-y-luz`,title:`A message unites different times`,lead:`Between stars, talking means sending information to a future you can't see. Transmitter power doesn't shorten that time distance.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A community sends a question to a star located a hundred light years away. The signal takes a century to arrive. If someone responds immediately, the answer needs another. Those who wrote the message will probably not participate in the response.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Interstellar communication makes dialogue a historic task. The meaning, language or method of interpretation must be retained during the interval. It can also change the receiving society between the moment we imagine it and the time when it receives the transmission.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Each distance opens a window to another date`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Looking at a star fifty light years away is seeing it fifty years ago. If you transmit an answer today, we will not receive it until within fifty others. In a simultaneous catalogue, each system appears in a different era: the near almost present and the far distant deeply ancient.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A civilization can go through visible phases as its light travels. We see perhaps the atmosphere before your industry, an emission that has already ended or a structure whose builders have changed. There is no astronomical map of the “now” shared.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The window influences what we are looking for. A supernova or predictable transit can serve as a common event to coordinate attention, but each observer receives it according to his position. The interstellar calendar needs to describe events and geometry, not assume synchronized watches.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Information also has a geometry`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A narrow beam concentrates energy, but requires knowing where the recipient will be. A wide emission covers more directions at the cost of reducing the energy received in each. SETI strategies consider this relationship between detectability and issuer design. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The light we observe shows a past. In estimating a future position, this delay must be combined with the movement of the system. Targeted optical pulses are a studied possibility, with specific time and targeted requirements. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`To aim, the emitter calculates where the receiver will be when the photons arrive. Uncertainty in one's own motion, planets or future orbits widens the area and distributes energy. A wider beam tolerates error but reduces intensity; a narrow one requires accurate prediction.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The same geometry affects chance encounters. A laser link between two worlds can sweep the Earth only during an alignment. Do not receive it later does not imply that it is turned off: the beam can continue to perform its function away from us.`},{kind:`note`,title:`Latency and amount of information`,paragraphs:[`One connection can transmit a lot of data per second and still have years of delay. Increasing bandwidth improves how much it arrives; it does not change the minimum propagation time. This difference determines which activities can be coordinated.`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`An encyclopedia, an art archive or a scientific description may be useful without immediate response. A daily conversation or remote control of a delicate operation is much more difficult. Communities would need local autonomy.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The message should teach you how to read it`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Recognising mathematical patterns can establish that a signal has structure, but does not translate references. A file could start with simple relationships, units defined by constants and examples that build a vocabulary. Still, moving from symbols to experiences, values, or irony would be difficult.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The receiver may not have the senses or concepts of the emitter. An image requires knowing dimensions and order of data; a measure needs unity; a story presupposes agents and time. The redundancy and multiple representations allow to verify interpretations, at the cost of bandwidth.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Answering involves institutions that survive those who received. They must preserve the original record, rival interpretations and the decision to transmit. An error can take centuries to correct. The conversation looks more like cooperative archaeology than a call.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Much information does not reduce waiting`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A powerful transmitter and a wide band can deliver large files when the signal arrives. They don't advance their first photon. Separate bandwidth and latency shows why an interstellar network can share libraries but not direct a distant ship in real time.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Each node would need to act on its own in the face of emergencies and political changes. The answers received belong to ancient questions; yet they can retain scientific and affective value. The message is addressed simultaneously to a place and an unknown generation.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Even recognizing a message does not guarantee understanding. The structure may suggest intent, but interpreting references requires context. The first exchanges could be devoted to building it slowly.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`There is something touching about this form of contact: send a signal whose reader has not yet been born. Light connects places and also generations. A civilization capable of speaking among stars would need to learn to entrust part of their conversations to future people.`}],sources:[{title:`Strategies and Advice for the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, 2021`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07283`},{title:`Optical SETI System Description`,publisher:`Harvard Optical SETI`,url:`https://seti.harvard.edu/oseti/targeted/system.htm`}],readingMinutes:4},{id:`deep-time`,title:`A Longer Work Than His Language of Origin`,lead:`Deep time changes what it means to finish, preserve and plan. Some hypothetical works would last longer than the cultures that named them.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The initial plane of an installation appears in a file. No one reads the language in which it was written anymore. One specialist reconstructs the units by comparing orbits drawn on the margins; another discovers that a word translated as "stable" actually meant "to review every thirty years". The work continues to be active, although each of its pieces has been replaced and its name has changed several times.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Deep time begins when duration ceases to be a long wait for a person and becomes a chain of relays. Astronomy works with processes of millions or billions of years: stars consume fuel, change structure and go through stages that no human observer can contemplate completely. Thinking about engineering on these scales requires designing continuity without knowing who will make the latest decisions. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Not all durations are the same. A block of material can persist, a machine can retain a function through repairs, an institution can sustain a program and a culture can remember why it started. Each layer can fail while the others survive. A hard ruin does not work; an automatic system works without anyone remembering its purpose; a community retains the purpose and completely replaces the artifact.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The work is delivered before it is finished`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A generation ship offers an intermediate scale to understand the problem. During a journey of centuries, the initial crew does not reach their destination. Their descendants must maintain propulsion, ecosystems, archives and industrial capabilities that they may never choose. The studies on world ships relate technical feasibility with population, organization, resources and duration; arriving does not depend solely on the helmet resisting. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Each relay receives an incomplete work and an explanation. To repair a pump you need plans, but you also need to know what symptoms matter, what assumptions guided the design and what changes were made afterwards. A manual that lists unexplained steps can become dangerous when the original replacement disappears. Transmitting knowledge means teaching to rebuild decisions, not just keeping instructions.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Training should be repeated even when no emergency occurs. Techniques used once a century are lost if no one practices them. A community could keep test benches, simulations and parts deliberately repairable for each generation to learn. It would also require multiple copies of the file, methods to detect alterations and physical references that allow recovery of drives even if conventions change.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Those who receive the project may not share their priorities. Perhaps a maneuver planned two hundred years ago today consumes a scarce resource. Maybe the destination revealed life and the mission must change. Designing a responsible relay requires leaving margins, alternatives and authority to stop. Continuity is not to obey eternally; it is to preserve sufficient capacity to understand, deliberate and act.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Maintaining the function transforms the object`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A long-lived machine loses atoms, components and configurations. Changing a radiator modifies its mass distribution; replacing controls requires translating interfaces; repairing a structure can introduce materials that designers did not know. After many cycles there is no clear boundary between original and replacement. Even so, the function can continue because each version delivers a verifiable status to the next one.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`That relief requires reference measures. Custodians can observe astronomical constants, store material samples, or describe experimental procedures for rebuilding scales. No documentary support is eternal: you have to copy, check and migrate the information before it fails. Migration, in turn, may lose details. That is why a deep file would need redundancy and independent explanations, not a single supposedly indestructible inscription.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The environment also does not wait still. Orbits suffer disturbances, surfaces receive impacts and stars slowly change their luminosity. An infrastructure must measure these variations and distinguish a temporary deviation from a secular trend. Keeping ‘the same' condition may require moving collectors, altering temperatures or leaving areas that were previously safe. The permanence of the function is more like continuous navigation than freezing a form.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`When the customer is a future star`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Proposals to intervene in stellar developments take the scale to the extreme. Mass extraction models, for example, explore how changing the mass of a star could modify its brightness and prolong certain stages of its life. These are theoretical research: they do not show that we can build infrastructure or that a society will support it. They do allow to identify physical relationships and compare scenarios under explicit assumptions. [3]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A slow intervention would have to respond to new observations. The expected extraction rate could become inadequate if models improve or if the star reacts in an unanticipated way. The instruments, the facility and the beneficiary population would change well before the cumulative effect reached its goal. In this context, "finishing" may mean keeping the system within a reviewable corridor, not reaching a configuration fixed at the beginning.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The reward also becomes strange. Those who bear the first costs would not see the remote benefit, and those who receive it could not thank them directly. The use of resources should be justified in the face of immediate needs without pretending to know the wishes of future people. Booking options can be more valuable than imposing a result on them: a removable or adjustable work delivers capacity in addition to an inheritance.`},{kind:`note`,title:`Not every prediction ages the same`,paragraphs:[`Physical laws allow certain processes to be projected when conditions and uncertainties are declared. A calculated orbit and a history of future institutions do not have the same reliability. The longer the term, the more necessary it is to separate what the physical model predicts from what the social narrative alone implies.`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Social uncertainty does not render all planning useless. Change what you want to promise. Inspections, pause thresholds and procedures for reviewing targets may be established. Material reserves make it possible to respond to unknown failures. The compartmentalization prevents a breakdown from destroying the whole. Recording disagreements helps a future custodian not to confuse an interim decision with a technical truth.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Meaning also needs maintenance`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Over the centuries, a work can acquire uses that its founders did not imagine. A warehouse becomes a shelter; a navigation beacon, a calendar; a stopped ship, a home. Preserving only the initial function could harm those who are now dependent on it. Deep maintenance includes recognizing these new relationships and deciding which ones deserve protection.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The opposite may also occur: the operation continues when its reason disappeared. A system extracts material because the order remains in force, even if there is no longer a project to receive it. The review points and the ability to safely shut down are forms of institutional intelligence. A machine that never asks does not represent perfect fidelity; it can represent the loss of all conversation with purpose.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`For a distant observer, these temporal layers would be difficult to separate. An abandoned structure can retain artificial geometry during ages. An active heat source may come from an old mechanism reused by recent inhabitants. Detecting a technosignature would indicate that some activity occurred, but it would not immediately reveal who initiated it, how long it lasted, or whether its meaning remained constant.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Let's go back to the file at the beginning. The specialist adds a note in her own language, includes the original data and explains why she corrected the translation. You know your format won't last either. Their contribution is not to write the definitive message, but to leave enough traces for another person to check and disagree. In deep time, a civilization does not show foresight by righting the whole future. It shows it by building relays in which those who do not yet exist receive knowledge, scope of action and permission to think again.`}],sources:[{title:`Stars`,publisher:`NASA Science`,url:`https://science.nasa.gov/universe/stars/`},{title:`World ships: Feasibility and Rationale`,publisher:`A. M. Hein, C. Smith, F. Marin y K. Staats, 2020`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2005.04100`},{title:`Lazarus Stars: Numerical investigations of stellar evolution with star-lifting as a life extension strategy`,publisher:`Matthew T. Scoggins y David Kipping, 2022; revisión de 2023`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.02338`}],readingMinutes:7},{id:`seti`,title:`Turn the question into an observation`,lead:`SETI looks for evidence of technology outside the Earth. His job is to transform a huge curiosity into evidence that an instrument can perform.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The control room doesn't hear voices on headphones. Receive data: frequencies, times, intensities and positions. Between interferences and natural phenomena, the team looks for patterns compatible with a technological origin and sufficiently concrete to verify them.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`SETI brings together diverse strategies. Radio is one of them; optical signals and consequences of technological activity are also sought. Some could be deliberate messages, other broadcasts accompanying an infrastructure. Choosing a search involves deciding what kind of phenomenon would be detectable. [1]`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Before the telescope there is a specific question`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A program can choose nearby stars, systems with known planets or large regions of the sky. The selection expresses a hypothesis: pointing to an exoplanet supposes that its location increases interest; sweeping the whole sky sacrifices time on each point to cover more directions. No list represents “the best places” without declared criteria.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Then a signal is chosen. A narrow radio line, an optical pulse or an infrared excess require different instruments and analysis. The team estimates what power would be visible at each distance, how much time to observe and what natural or human phenomena could imitate it. Only then does the cosmic question become an experiment with the possibility of failure.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Observation begins by calibrating. Known sources allow checking sensitivity, frequency and clock. Instrument and environment conditions are recorded because a cable, satellite or discharge into the detector can produce the most striking event of the night.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`A signal has more dimensions than a direction`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Watching a star doesn't mean having checked everything it could emit. They import the frequency band, duration, time resolution, sensitivity and timing of observation. The SETI search space analysis shows why null results have a limited scope. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`An emitter could be intermittent or point somewhere else. There may also be technology that our instruments do not recognize. These possibilities do not prove that someone is transmitting; they explain why a search must declare its conditions.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The raw data is often too large for manual inspection. Algorithms identify events that exceed thresholds, group similar shapes and compare when the telescope points to the target or departs. That reduction should retain enough information to review errors: an overly aggressive filter can erase the signal that did not look like expected.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A candidate acquires a tab: coordinates, instant, frequency or wavelength, intensity, duration, drift and receiver configuration. With it another team can try to reproduce the observation. Without those metadata, an interesting mark is just a disconnected image of the sky.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Exciting starts with checking`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A candidate signal needs to be checked at other times and, where possible, with independent instruments. They must be proven, ground interferences must be ruled out and natural hypotheses must be contrasted. A striking graph is the beginning of a work, not its conclusion.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`In optical, for example, some instruments compare quick detections to distinguish a real pulse from internal events from the detector. The experimental design incorporates the question of how he could deceive himself. [3]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The first discard asks if the signal follows the telescope or the observatory. A local interference can appear in many directions; a celestial source should relate to a position. Catalogues of satellites, transmitters and astronomical phenomena are then reviewed. The extraterrestrial technological explanation gains interest only when concrete alternatives lose strength.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Repeat help, but the absence of repetition does not decide alone. The emitter could turn off or the first event would be noise. Monitoring is scheduled at different times and, if possible, from separate locations. Two observatories that see the same direction simultaneously reduce many local causes.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Publishing a result is also part of the method`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A team should describe selection, sensitivity, filters and sufficient data for others to evaluate the limit. Publishing only striking candidates would produce a biased image of the work. The well-characterised null results show which emitters would have been detected and help design subsequent campaigns.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`If a persistent signal appeared, confirming technological origin would not yet be equivalent to translating a message. First it would be necessary to establish provenance, repetition and physical properties. Cultural interpretation would come later, with greater uncertainty. Separate discovery and interpretation protects the result from our expectations.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`A Search is a Time Decision`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Observing many stars once favors frequent signals; returning for years favors intermittances and allows checking repetition. Dedicating great resolution to one band reduces resources for others. The programmes complement each other precisely because they make different commitments.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Scientific patience appears when an exciting candidate turns out to be a satellite and is documented equally. Discarding improves catalogs and filters. It was not a lost night: the instrument learned to recognize an earthly voice that could be confused with stars before.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Priorities can be reviewed when a new planet appears, a receiver improves, or systematic interference is discovered. A cumulative program retains data in reusable formats and declares method changes. Thus, an old observation can be analyzed with a new question without pretending that the instrument recorded what it could never measure. The continuity of the file extends the search time beyond a campaign.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`At dawn, the room doesn't have a message, but it does have a shorter list of explanations for each candidate and a better defined limit. That is the daily scale of discovery: turning amazement into procedures that another person can repeat.`},{kind:`note`,title:`A Well Measured Absence Does Teach`,paragraphs:[`If a search would have detected certain emitters and found none, it limits their abundance under those assumptions. It does not automatically extend the conclusion to all powers, epochs, directions and technologies.`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The human dimension appears in patience. A person can dedicate his career to improving a sensitivity or discard false positives without witnessing a contact. That work expands what we can say about heaven.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`SETI makes an old question admit verifiable results. The universe is not obliged to respond with an understandable message. Even so, each well-designed instrument makes a small part of our uncertainty a real observation.`}],sources:[{title:`Strategies and Advice for the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, 2021`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07283`},{title:`How Much SETI Has Been Done? Finding Needles in the n-Dimensional Cosmic Haystack`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, S. Kanodia y E. Lubar, 2018`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07252`},{title:`Optical SETI System Description`,publisher:`Harvard Optical SETI`,url:`https://seti.harvard.edu/oseti/targeted/system.htm`}],readingMinutes:5},{id:`technosignatures`,title:`Technology can leave footprints without sending greetings`,lead:`A technosignature would be an observable sign of technological activity. Recognizing it requires explaining why this activity fits better than alternatives.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A distant observer may not distinguish buildings or machines and yet measure a consequence of their operation. That's what the technosignatures suggest: the activity can extend its observable reach well beyond the size of the piece that produces it.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The search includes deliberate signals and side effects. A narrow transmission, a luminous pulse or a particular thermal distribution present different observations. There is no universal detector of civilizations. [1]`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Each family demands a different suspicion`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Communication signals are sought by their concentration in frequency, time or direction. A very narrow radio carrier can be difficult to produce naturally; a short laser pulse concentrates photons. In both cases it must be demonstrated that it came from heaven, exclude interference and establish whether it repeats.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Atmospheric footprints work differently. A chemical combination might suggest industry if it remains out of balance, but first geology, biology, star and spectroscopic errors should be modeled. A molecule made on Earth is not automatically artificial on another planet.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Large infrastructures would be sought through transits, concealments or energy balance. Unusual shapes in a curve of light admit dust, fragments and star variability. A thermal excess supports discs and regions of star formation. The larger the scale claimed, the more detailed the natural comparison should be.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The print has to belong to something.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`An infrared excess can motivate the hypothesis of large pickers, but it also has natural explanations. Dyson's models help predict what energy would be absorbed and reissued, then compare that prediction with dust and other astrophysic components. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`An optical signal requires another check chain. Detectors, temporary coincidences and follow-up observations allow you to ask if the pulse came from the sky and what properties it had. Its brief or intense appearance does not determine its origin on its own. [3]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Local artifacts may also persist: a probe, processed material, or lighting in a nearby world. Searching the Solar System allows more detail, but covers a huge territory and must distinguish human technology, geology and image noise. The proximity changes the method, does not guarantee ease.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A technosignature can be intentional or accidental. This difference affects duration and geometry, but is not directly observed as an emotion. First the signal is measured; then architectures capable of producing it are evaluated. Attributing a desire for contact before knowing the mechanism reverses reasoning.`},{kind:`note`,title:`Prediction before label`,paragraphs:[`A useful technological hypothesis says what else we should observe: repetition, spectrum, evolution or a relationship between signals. If any result can be accommodated by inventing another extraterrestrial intention, the proposal loses ability to be contrasted.`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The duration of a print also matters. A message can pass in seconds; an infrastructure or its waste could persist much more. Looking for ancient technology expands the possible time coincidence, although it complicates rebuilding its cause.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Several footprints can hold each other`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A case would gain strength if independent predictions match. A planet with abnormal chemistry, periodic lighting and tight emissions deserves more attention than any of those isolated signals. The combination should be carefully planned so as not to select only the favorable data afterwards.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The time scale helps to sort out follow-ups. A pulse calls for rapid reaction; an atmosphere can be observed for years; an orbital structure reveals periodicity in many transits. Each campaign keeps different metadata and needs complementary tools.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The most common result will be a limit. You can say how many systems lack transmitters above a certain power or what fraction of galaxies do not show certain thermal excess. That language maintains a precise link between observation and hypothesis.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Natural explanations are not administrative obstacles: they are scientific models that can also reveal disks, variable stars or unexpected planetary chemistry. A technosignatures campaign learns from the universe even when technology loses comparison. Only after exhausting alternatives with new predictions would it make sense to elevate artificial interpretation.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Imagine that the first clue is not a phrase, but an energy balance that does not fit. New observations would be needed before attributing it to someone. The discovery, if it came, would be built with physical consequences until a technological explanation gained strength.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The question changes its tone: besides asking if anyone wants to talk, we can ask what would leave the simple fact of doing things in the universe.`}],sources:[{title:`Strategies and Advice for the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, 2021`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07283`},{title:`Dyson Spheres`,publisher:`Jason T. Wright, 2020`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.16734`},{title:`Optical SETI System Description`,publisher:`Harvard Optical SETI`,url:`https://seti.harvard.edu/oseti/targeted/system.htm`}],readingMinutes:4},{id:`radio-seti`,title:`Find a line between millions of frequencies`,lead:`The radio allows us to study weak signals with great precision. The closest challenge is often to separate the sky from our own transmitters.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`In a data representation, time occupies one axis and frequency another. A persistent signal could draw a line; if it changes its apparent frequency, the line bends. The figure seems simple, but there are many reasons why it may appear.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Some SETI searches are interested in very narrow band signals, compatible with artificial emitters. They also examine frequency changes associated with relative movement. The choice defines what signals would be found and what would be left out. [1]`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The spectrum becomes a moving image`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The receiver separates a band on many channels and repeats the measurement over time. On the spectrogram, a stable carrier draws a line. The rotation and orbit of the emitter or telescope changes the frequency received by Doppler effect, so that a celestial source can tilt. The software tests different drift rates to recover energy that, without correction, would be distributed.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Narrower channels concentrate a narrow signal against noise, but multiply calculation and data. Integrate longer improves sensitivity for something persistent; a short transmission is diluted or disappears. Resolution, duration and drift are chosen together.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The bandwidth also contains physical information. Natural phenomena often extend energy by frequencies, although there are narrow astronomical sources and human emissions in many ways. “Artificial” is never decided by a single width: it arises from a set of properties and discards.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The Earth speaks too close`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Satellites, communications and electronics generate interference. A candidate can be intense precisely because he is close. Comparing observations towards the objective and beyond, reviewing other instruments and studying temporal evolution helps to distinguish origins.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Doppler drift alone does not demonstrate an interstellar origin. Both the receiver and possible nearby transmitters move. The analysis needs a set of consistent properties, plus a nice line on the screen.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A regular test alternates between the target and nearby positions. A signal that remains when the antenna departs probably enters by interference or lateral lobes. Another one that appears only on the star deserves follow-up. Several separate radio telescopes can check if the event has the delay and direction compatible with the sky.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Satellite catalogues and assigned bands help, but the environment changes. Aircraft, spacecraft and defective equipment produce unexpected emissions. Recording the time accurately allows you to reconstruct who was on the horizon.`},{kind:`note`,title:`Listening finer has a cost`,paragraphs:[`Splitting spectrum into narrow channels may favor certain signals, but it requires processing more data and considering their changes during integration. A signal moving between channels may be lost if the procedure does not contemplate this drift.`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The power needed to detect an emitter depends on distance, directionality and sensitivity. An antenna that points to a beam concentrates energy, but also reduces the probability that we are in its path. It can't be assumed that any alien communication would illuminate the entire galaxy.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The limits obtained belong to a specific search space. Checking a band for one night doesn't mean listening to an entire star forever. [2]`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Repeat changes candidate category`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`If the line returns from the same position with a compatible drift, periodicity and movement can be estimated. Switching telescope checks hardware; observing control sources checks the procedure. Confirmation needs a data string, not a memorable capture.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A non-repetition preserves ambiguity. It can be a single impulse, missing interference or error. The team publishes comparable windows conditions and program. The more specific the original search was, the more precise it will be that a follow-up can refute.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Directionality alters expectations. A lighthouse sweeping the sky could return with a period; a link between two planets only reaches us during alignments; a weak escape demands proximity. The equivalent isotropic power allows to compare detectability, but does not reveal what the antenna was like.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Polarisation and dispersion can provide additional controls. The material between stars modifies signals of frequency-dependent forms, while a local source crosses another path. No isolated property certifies distance, but together they allow rebuilding if the wave behaved like something that crossed the interstellar medium.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`There is a strange intimacy in this work: a large antenna collects tiny variations of the electromagnetic field. If one had distant technological origin, it would have crossed years of darkness to end up as a sequence of numbers that someone decides to review once again.`}],sources:[{title:`Strategies and Advice for the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, 2021`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07283`},{title:`How Much SETI Has Been Done? Finding Needles in the n-Dimensional Cosmic Haystack`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, S. Kanodia y E. Lubar, 2018`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07252`}],readingMinutes:4},{id:`optical-seti`,title:`A flash shorter than a blink`,lead:`A laser signal could stand out for a tiny moment. To find it, the telescope must also observe in the right time.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A camera that exposes for several seconds mixes all the light received in that interval. An extremely short flash can be diluted. SETI optical scans instruments capable of recognizing fast pulses or spectral features compatible with technological emissions.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The system described by Harvard for optical pulses uses rapid detection and coincidences to distinguish actual events from instrumental noise. The experimental question is concrete: what combination of photons, duration and response of the detector would count as candidate. [1]`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Two detectors must agree.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A photomultiplier transforms the arrival of photons into electric pulses. It also produces internal events and receives energy particles. Splitting the light between independent detectors allows you to demand coincidence within a very short window: a celestial pulse should reach both, while many defects affect only one.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The threshold balances sensitivity and false alarms. Too high loses weak transmissions; too low fills the noise record. Background rates and response to known sources are measured before observing. The clock is part of the instrument because a nanosecond match depends on synchronization.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Another strategy looks for persistent laser lines in a spectrum. There they matter resolution and contrast in front of the star, not just temporal velocity. “SETI optical” thus combines pulses and continuous emissions with different analysis pipes.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The star doesn't need to shut down.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A targeted laser can concentrate energy at a small angle and in a short time. Under certain conditions, a pulse could stand out from the received background of a star. This does not imply that its average power continuously exceeds all stellar brightness.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Pointing well would be essential for the issuer. At interstellar distances, the recipient moves during the journey of light. A deliberate transmission would need to predict where it will be, or sweep a larger region by paying for another energy cost.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The Earth's atmosphere absorbs and disperses part of the light, and can produce apparent flashes. Satellites reflect the Sun; meteors and stellar phenomena change brightness. A candidate retains time, position, pulse profile and sky state to compare those alternatives.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`An instrument with a small field observes few stars with great sensitivity. A wide camera covers more sky at the cost of collecting more background or less detail. The probability of matching a brief beam depends on both the transmitter and that observational choice.`},{kind:`note`,title:`Duration and average power`,paragraphs:[`If a pulse contains energy E and lasts Δt, its power during the pulse is approximately E/Δt. The average power depends on how much it repeats. Confounding both figures exaggerates or underestimates the transmitter requirement.`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A candidate must be contrasted with natural phenomena, detector events and human sources. Repetition would be valuable, but a search can also record unique events and retain information for analysis. SETI strategies vary in coverage and sensitivity. [2]`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Follow-up looks for geometry`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`If the pulse is repeated, other observatories can measure whether it comes from the same coordinate and if it follows the celestial movement. Regular repetitions could suggest a headlamp; intervals linked to an orbit would offer another clue. These interpretations come after establishing that the detector did not make them.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A single pulse cannot be called again. Saving auxiliary data allows you to review airplanes, satellites, cosmic rays and climate. Publishing an event without that information increases emotion and reduces knowledge.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The technical possibility of the laser also does not demonstrate intention. An energy beam can serve communication, propulsion or measurement and cross our line of vision by accident. Recognizing technology and understanding its use would be separate discoveries.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The chosen wavelength modifies atmospheric absorption, star brightness and detector efficiency. A search selects windows where the sky and instrument allow good contrast, and states which pulses would be left out. Space observatories would avoid part of the atmosphere, but they would have area limits, operation and data. Combining separate terrestrial sites improves confirmation without turning the entire sky into continuous surveillance.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`When two detectors match, the screen retains only one mark with time and energy. The whole scene of the supposed transmitter is still missing. The rigor consists of not filling it before checking where the light came from.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Perhaps a sign passes through the Earth while almost everyone sleeps, too brief to form an image in the eyes. The possibility of recognizing it would depend on clocks, detectors and an instrument you were already looking at. In this form of search, expanding our attention means learning to perceive intervals that human experience normally melts into one.`}],sources:[{title:`Optical SETI System Description`,publisher:`Harvard Optical SETI`,url:`https://seti.harvard.edu/oseti/targeted/system.htm`},{title:`Strategies and Advice for the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, 2021`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07283`}],readingMinutes:4},{id:`fermi`,title:`An ancient sky and a question still open`,lead:`Fermi's question arises from a tension: if other civilizations could appear and spread, why do we not have clear evidence of them?`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`You look at the Milky Way from a dark place. Each region contains stars with longer stories than humanity, some far older than the Sun. It is natural to imagine that somewhere, someone could have had a huge temporal advantage. Then comes the question: if that happened, what should we see?`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Fermi's so-called paradox is not a proven contradiction between two firm facts. It combines limited observations with assumptions about the origin of life, technology, its duration and consequences. Tension grows when we frequently give several stages that we do not yet know how to measure well.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The chain has many unknown links`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A suitable planet must originate or receive life. That life may or may not produce complex organisms and technological capabilities. A society can last little, change its interests or not make interstellar expansion. Each transition introduces uncertainty.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Sandberg, Drexler and Ord discussed how addressing broad uncertainties in these odds changes the conclusions. His work shows why replacing unknown amounts with seemingly reasonable values can hide very different possibilities, including a great rarity. It doesn't say we're alone. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`We also don't know a law that forces all technology to expand. But to claim that no society would need as much caution as to say that they would all. With enough opportunities, a few expansive trajectories could matter a lot.`},{kind:`note`,title:`A multiplication can hide orders of magnitude`,paragraphs:[`If several unknown odds multiply, changing each modestly can change the result a lot. Using a single figure for each factor gives an apparent accuracy that the data does not support. It is important to explore ranges and dependencies, not just a central result.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Not having found it doesn't mean looking at everything.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Technological searches cover specific directions, frequencies, sensitivities and durations. The work on the SETI search space compares that coverage with a much larger set of possibilities. A null result restricts certain emitters, not all conceivable activities. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`That doesn't make the search useless. On the contrary: a well-defined search allows you to learn even when you do not find candidates. The question is to keep the observation together and its scope.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`We might not coincide in time with a broadcasting phase. A society could emit little to us or leave traces we haven't looked for. There might also be no fence. Observations should reduce that range rather than selecting in advance the most dramatic story.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`A busy galaxy can be irregular`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Settlement simulations explore how travel, preparation times and available destinations produce different distributions. Some allow unoccupied regions or communities to disappear while others continue. This complicates the image of an expansion that inevitably reaches every system and remains there forever. [3]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The model doesn't prove that we live in a hole. It makes it possible to ask a more precise question: under what parameters a gap like this would be likely and what other signals we would expect.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The distinction also applies to the zoo, the dark forest and the large filter. They are frameworks for reasoning, with very different assumptions. They do not form a collection of confirmed responses from which it is sufficient to choose a favorite.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Silence also does not describe intentions`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The absence of a message does not allow us to attribute fear, hostility or indifference. These are possible explanations within broader stories, but require additional evidence. Projecting human psychology over millions of unknown societies does not turn uncertainty into knowledge.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`SETI strategies offer a practical way out: to look for specific phenomena and improve how they are checked. Each measurement may rule out a class of hypothesis or indicate a new observation required. [4]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Fermi's strength is that the question unites scales. A human night looks at processes that could last billions of years. We still don't know if the sky seems silent because technological life is rare, because its footprints are hard to recognize or because of a combination we just started exploring.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Looking back does not require solving it in advance. Astonishment can be sustained in that exact situation: there are enough reasons to ask and still too little to give the answer.`}],sources:[{title:`Dissolving the Fermi Paradox`,publisher:`A. Sandberg, E. Drexler y T. Ord, 2018`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.02404`},{title:`How Much SETI Has Been Done? Finding Needles in the n-Dimensional Cosmic Haystack`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, S. Kanodia y E. Lubar, 2018`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07252`},{title:`The Fermi Paradox and the Aurora Effect: Exo-civilization Settlement, Expansion and Steady States`,publisher:`Carroll-Nellenback, Frank, Wright y Scharf, 2019`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.04450`},{title:`Strategies and Advice for the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, 2021`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07283`}],readingMinutes:4},{id:`great-filter`,title:`The difficulty could be behind or in front`,lead:`The great filter organizes an uncertainty about our history: what transitions make it rare to get from lifeless matter to a lasting and visible civilization.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Imagine a sequence of doors. Some represent the origin of life; others represent biological changes, technology and long-term continuity. The metaphor does not force the existence of a single almost impossible door. Several accumulated difficulties could greatly reduce the number of trajectories reaching the end.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Hanson formulated the great filter as a way of thinking about that reduction. It is an argument about probabilities and observations, not the discovery of a universal catastrophe that awaits every civilization. [1]`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What we've been through can also be weird.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`If an early transition were extraordinarily difficult, our existence would not prove that the technological future is necessarily short. If the initial stages were common and the lasting expansion rare, it would be necessary to investigate what explains the difference. But we still don't know the odds or their dependencies well.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A discovery of simple life outside the Earth would be relevant, especially if it proved an independent origin. Their interpretation would depend on what was found and where. It would not allow us to jump directly to a precise prediction about our destiny.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Analyses that incorporate broad uncertainties in the factors of emergence of civilizations show how much conclusions can change compared to point calculations. [2]`},{kind:`note`,title:`The bias of being here to ask`,paragraphs:[`Only observers who have gone through certain stages can measure their history from within. This makes it difficult to infer the ease of these stages only from a successful example. A transition that occurred on Earth does not have to be frequent on all the right worlds.`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The framework may motivate the study of real risks, but it does not replace that study. Attributing any danger to the large filter does not calculate its probability or indicate how to reduce it. Each risk requires specific evidence and mechanisms.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Before the animals there were many doors`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The first candidate is at the origin of life. The young Earth went from non-living chemistry to systems capable of inheritance and evolution, but we do not know all the steps or their probability in other environments. That life appeared relatively soon here can be informative, although the exact date and bias of observing from a world where it did occur make it difficult to turn a single case into cosmic frequency.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Then come transitions that took a long time: photosynthesis, complex cells, multicellularity and organisms with cumulative learning. Some innovations appeared several times and others seem to have few known origins. This history formulates candidates, but an earthly rarity may reflect difficulty, contingency, or loss of evidence.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Intelligence and technology also do not form a single step. Cognitive abilities, cultural communication, energy, materials and institutions that retain knowledge are needed. A planet can have abundant life without producing radio astronomy. Another can produce technology without expanding or issuing recognizable signals.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The filter can be a sum. Several moderately unlikely transitions greatly reduce the result without an absolutely impossible door. They may also depend on each other: oxygen, geology, climate and evolution are not separate draws. Multiplying unknown figures offers a precision that we do not yet possess.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`In front of it there is no catastrophe identified`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A late filter could include failure of technological societies before reaching detectable duration or expansion. War, environmental degradation, poorly controlled technologies and institutional loss are different mechanisms. None are likely to occupy a place in metaphor; each requires data, causal models and self-prevention.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`There could also be no destruction. Perhaps few societies choose to expand, their visible phases are brief or their activity is difficult to recognize. The rare transition would then be of conduct or detectability. Astronomical silence does not automatically translate into extinction.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Each finding would move a different part`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Finding microbes on Mars would mean different things according to their origin. If they shared ancestry with earthly life, they could come from exchanged material. An independent genesis would indicate that the first transition occurred twice in a system and would make it less plausible as a universal barrier.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`An exoplanetary biosphere would add another case when the biological signal and environment were well established. Complex life would narrow down later stages. A confirmed technosignature would prove that another trajectory achieved observable technology; knowing its antiquity would provide information about continuity. No individual discovery alone would indicate where the entire filter is.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Absence reports within a defined search. Not finding radio in certain stars limits transmitters of certain frequencies, powers and moments. It does not eliminate microbial oceans or technologies outside such coverage. Analyses with broad uncertainties show how much conclusions change compared to ad hoc calculations. [2]`},{kind:`heading`,text:`A tool to ask better`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The frame prevents mixing stages. Abiogenesis belongs to planetary chemistry; complexity, evolution; technological duration, risks and institutions; visibility, astronomy and engineering. Each discipline can reduce unsolved uncertainty.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`We only know that the whole chain happened at least once because we're here. We do not see from outside the earth roads that could fail, nor can we repeat history to measure frequencies. Finding other biospheres and technosignatures is the way to start a comparison.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The sequence also does not necessarily end with galactic expansion. A durable society can stay local, transform little energy or direct its signals away from us. If the observed result requires “visible civilization”, then engineering decisions appear within the chain along with biological transitions. Defining the result avoids calling failure any trajectory that does not resemble a human expectation.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`In a lab room, a Martian sample with an independent biochemistry would change a column from that huge table. I wouldn't reveal the others. That modesty is precisely progress: replacing a door imagined by a better delimited probability and a new verifiable question.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`As those data arrive, the frame must keep several open routes. Its function is to order ignorance and orient measurements, not produce fatalism with a nonexistent statistic.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The image of the doors is disturbing because we do not see how many are left. It can also orient curiosity backwards: understanding terrestrial life and searching for other biospheres would help to know the path that made this conversation possible. The future remains open, and the metaphor should not close it before the data.`}],sources:[{title:`The Great Filter — Are We Almost Past It?`,publisher:`Robin Hanson, ensayo`,url:`https://mason.gmu.edu/~rhanson/greatfilter.html`},{title:`Dissolving the Fermi Paradox`,publisher:`A. Sandberg, E. Drexler y T. Ord, 2018`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.02404`}],readingMinutes:5},{id:`zoo-hypothesis`,title:`A silence someone would have chosen`,lead:`The zoo hypothesis imagines that other civilizations avoid interfering with us. Their difficulty is to turn that supposed intention into evidence.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The sky looks the same, but it changes the interpretation: stars could host observers who know the Earth and prefer to stay away. That is the intuition of the zoo hypothesis, posed by John Ball as a possible explanation of the absence of obvious contact. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The image is based on a human experience: sometimes we observe ecosystems trying not to alter them. Moving it to other civilizations may be suggestive, but it does not show that they share our reasons or that they can coordinate such conduct during immense times.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Who would keep the deal?`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`If there were many independent societies, it would be necessary to explain why all respected non-interference or what limited exceptions. If only one has scope over us, there is another question: how do we know it exists? Adding invisible rules can make a story coherent without making it more verifiable.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A version that attributes to observers the perfect ability to hide any signal is especially difficult to contrast. Each absence fits by definition. In order to advance scientifically, it would be necessary to propose some consequence that could distinguish that scenario from a universe without close visitors.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Watching without touching also leaves traces`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`To isolate a society, someone would have to detect it and follow its development. A beacon could observe the Earth, but sending data and receiving instructions takes time. In thousands of light years, a decision based on our first emissions would come when millennia had passed here. The policy would need local rules capable of acting with old information.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Observatories require energy, communications, orbital corrections and maintenance. They could be located far away or use narrow beams, but each solution has possible traces. Hiding the activity of many societies in our sky would also be a different and far greater task than avoiding direct contact.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A modest version would explain nearby visitors who do not land or transmit to us. It does not automatically explain the lack of distant galactic technosignatures, whose photons departed before technological humanity existed. The hypothesis must state what silence it tries to explain.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The agreement would have thresholds and exceptions`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`If there are many independent societies, they would all have to know the reserve and limit themselves to explorers during enormous times. An exception would be enough to break the silence. A dominant guardian would resolve coordination, but he adds a galactic power whose existence is not established either.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Criteria would also be needed: does isolation end with agriculture, radio, space travel or an explicit request? Even failure to intervene has consequences if observers are aware of a preventable catastrophe. The word zoo compresses political and moral decisions that can produce disagreement.`},{kind:`note`,title:`Consistency is not evidence`,paragraphs:[`An explanation may be logically possible and lack observational support. Their ability to narrate silence does not measure how much more likely it is in the face of other explanations. Such a comparison requires explicit assumptions and data.`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`SETI can continue to search for concrete signals without adopting in advance the idea of a cosmic reserve. If a technosignature appears, its analysis must start from the measured, not from the intention that most intrigates us. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A contrastable version would have to risk failing: predicting artifacts, communication patterns, or a change at a certain threshold. If each result is attributed to guardians capable of making any appearance, it cannot be distinguished from a universe without them.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Costs should also be compared. Keeping observation for millions of years consumes infrastructure and forces the rule to be passed on to successors who may no longer share its purpose. A stable agreement would require auditing, dissident management and some response to errors: a probe might accidentally reveal its presence or confuse a natural signal with a request. The more detail is added, the more potentially observable consequences appear.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`If an observer intervened only to prevent catastrophes, he would leave another kind of pattern: repeated coincidences between dangers and corrections. Proposing it would require defining in advance what events count, because selecting coincidences after seeing them would allow to accommodate any human history.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The most disturbing scene is a remote room where an image of an ancient Earth arrives. Those who observe must decide with inherited rules what it means to take care of us. The scene develops the responsibility required by the proposal; it is not evidence that this room exists.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The hypothesis has a powerful literary effect: it turns our apparent loneliness into a possible decision by others. This investment should be enjoyed without forgetting its status. Until it produces differentiating evidence, it teaches us more about how we imagine observation and intervention than about confirmed inhabitants of heaven.`}],sources:[{title:`The zoo hypothesis`,publisher:`John A. Ball, Icarus, 1973`,url:`https://doi.org/10.1016/0019-1035(73)90111-5`},{title:`Strategies and Advice for the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, 2021`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07283`}],readingMinutes:4},{id:`dark-forest`,title:`When imagining the other produces fear`,lead:`The dark forest turns uncertainty among civilizations into a logic of concealment. It is a powerful narrative idea, not a proven law of cosmic life.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`In a nocturnal forest, a noise can reveal a presence without explaining its intentions. The metaphor moved into the universe suggests societies that avoid announcing themselves because they don't know what to respond to. Cixin Liu develops it in her novel The Dark Forest. Its literary provenance is an essential part of how it should be read. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The reasoning imagines enormous distances, uncertainty about the other and potentially irreversible consequences. Under certain assumptions about capabilities and interests, hiding may seem attractive. The unjustified leap would be to turn these assumptions into a universal psychology.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The chain of fear has several links`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`First, survival is supposed to dominate targets. Then, no one can know other people's intentions or trust them to remain stable. It adds that capabilities can grow to make a society once weak dangerous. Finally, a preventive attack must be possible and advantageous. Only with the whole chain hiding or attacking seems dominant.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Each link admits alternatives. A society can value cooperation or knowledge. Distance makes promises difficult, but also project strength. An attack points to ancient information and upon arrival may find another power, an empty place or defenses. He also reveals the aggressor to observers that they may have ignored him.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Light imposes delays. A hundred light years away, observing and responding takes centuries. There is no quick negotiation, but there is no instant threat. The strategy depends on speed, cost, detectability and attribution of weapons, not just mistrust.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Shutting up doesn't guarantee disappearing.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Turn off wide transmitters does not erase industry, matter movement or heat. Hiding them may require giving up activities or controlling difficult emissions. The dark forest mainly deals with a communicative decision, while SETI contemplates other technosignatures.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`In addition, our searches cover a small fraction of frequencies, directions, powers and times. The “cosmic pay” quantifies that limited coverage. [2] Before attributing absence to fear it would be necessary to show that the expected signals were within what was observed.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Silence admits many stories`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Failure to detect a signal may also be due to sensitivity, timing, direction or absence of emitters. Limited search coverage prevents attributing the result to a deliberate decision to hide. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Nor do we know that preventive attack is always possible, effective or preferred. The distance that makes it difficult to trust also makes it difficult to know, reach and predict the other. A strategic argument needs to specify those costs, rather than taking them out for a sufficiently advanced civilization.`},{kind:`note`,title:`A model depends on its players`,paragraphs:[`Changing objectives, available information or consequences alters a strategic conclusion. If every society is supposed to prioritize the same thing and evaluates uncertainty equally, this uniformity is part of the model; it is not an astronomical data.`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The discussion of deliberate communications may merit attention, but it must be distinguished from passive pursuit and supported by concrete arguments. SETI studies observable signals; it does not require adopting a single explanation of the intentions of potential emitters. [3]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Deliberately conveying raises who decides, what scope the message has and what scenarios justify caution. There are intermediate options: observe first, use directed beams or agree internal criteria. The metaphor formulates a concern; it does not calculate the probability of harm or prove that absolute silence is safe.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Cooperation also has possible mechanisms. Repeated messages allow consistency to be verified; commitments can be built through gradual exchanges; scientific information can be shared before sensitive coordinates. Centuries of delay make all this fragile, but not impossible by definition. A strategic analysis should compare the expected costs of cooperating, quieting and attacking under target distributions, rather than assigning certainty to the worst case.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`In addition, hiding can fail once and lose its benefit. A biosphere alters its atmosphere during ages and a technology can be inferred by consequences not intended as messages. The strategy of the forest then depends on when surveillance began and what signals already cross space.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`An old transmission cannot recover once its luminous front expands. Even a silent society today was able to announce its chemistry, industry or radar to observers in the right direction. The forest would have physical memory: past decisions limit the present concealment.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The metaphor leaves a lasting sensation: stars turned into possible watchful windows. We can explore that emotion without confusing it with evidence. The dark sky remains a place where we know little, and fear is one of our answers to that lack of information, not a measurement of what is there.`}],sources:[{title:`The Dark Forest`,publisher:`Cixin Liu; novela de ciencia ficción, editorial Macmillan`,url:`https://us.macmillan.com/books/9781250430663/thedarkforest/`},{title:`How Much SETI Has Been Done? Finding Needles in the n-Dimensional Cosmic Haystack`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, S. Kanodia y E. Lubar, 2018`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07252`},{title:`Strategies and Advice for the Search for Extraterrestrial Intelligence`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, 2021`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2107.07283`}],readingMinutes:4},{id:`grabby-aliens`,title:`Borders Growing Through the Universe`,lead:`The model of expansionary civilizations asks how the cosmic map changes when some societies transform growing regions.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Imagine an animation of the universe over billions of years. At first only isolated points appear. Some remain small; others originate regions that grow, meet and leave a border between different stories. The drawing looks like bubbles spreading, but does not represent observed ships. It is the departure of a model that asks what an observer would see before an expansive region reached its place.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Hanson and collaborators call *grabby* hypothetical civilizations that expand to a appreciable fraction of the speed of light, produce visible changes on a large scale and prevent other independent expansionary civilizations from appearing within their domain. The term describes those model rules. It does not necessarily mean aggression, military conquest or a collective mind. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The problem they're trying to study comes from our calendar. The Earth appeared late for the first stars, but humanity seems early if the universe will continue to form habitable environments for a very long time. Why are we watching now? The model explores a conditional response: perhaps observers like us can only emerge before expansionary fronts transform their region.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Three assumptions produce the map`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`First, an emergence rate is assumed for civilizations with expansive capacity. This rate can vary with cosmic time by following conditions such as star formation, planets and possible evolutionary delays. We do not know that distribution from a sample of civilizations; a mathematical form is proposed and its consequences are examined.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Second, an effective speed is assigned to expansion. It's not just the maximum speed of a ship. Includes travel, settlement construction and time before launching the next stage. An idealized border sums up all these local operations in a number. More detailed settlement models show that stellar movement, available destinations and community duration can create much more irregular maps. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Third, the transformed domain is supposed to exclude new independent appearances of the same type. Perhaps an already occupied region modifies planets or uses resources; the model does not need to decide the exact mechanism. But such exclusion is crucial: without it, the temporary position of an observer is not filtered out in the same way.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`By combining the three assumptions, early regions gain volume and reduce the space where unincorporated observers can emerge. Borders end up meeting. Calculus can relate speeds, moments of appearance and expected separations, always within that simplified universe.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Our position would not be a random sample.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Suppose you choose any point from the current space. It could be inside a transformed region. We know that we do not observe from a place recognized as such. This information conditions the sample: we belong, by definition of the scenario, to all of the places not yet reached.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The effect seems to ask about the length of a room by interviewing only people who are still inside. The stays that ended earlier do not appear in the survey. Similarly, if expansion blocks new independent observers, those who may wonder about their apparent precocity come from areas and times that survived the filter. This is an observer selection application, not direct evidence that the filter exists.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Nor does reasoning show that we are typical among permitted observers. To infer parameters you need to decide how to treat our date of appearance within the distribution: as a more or less common observation, for example. Changing that choice alters the results. The conclusion retains both the supposed physics and the statistical rule used to locate us.`},{kind:`note`,title:`A parameter is not a measured prediction`,paragraphs:[`A speed, distance or date calculated by the model does not amount to an astronomical measurement. It can be an inference conditioned by the rate of appearance, the rule of exclusion and how we consider our position. Modifying any one of them produces another statistical universe.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`What would I try to observe?`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The model incorporates visibility: a relevant expansion should change large regions in a recognizable way. In principle we could look for energy or spectral alterations in sets of stars and compare their distribution with natural explanations. Finding a dark area or a strange galaxy is not enough. It would require a definite signature, instrumental controls and contrasted astrophysical alternatives.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Not detecting nearby borders may restrict some combinations of appearance, speed and visibility, but does not confirm the remaining ones. There may not be expansionary civilizations, they may be very rare, operate in a less visible way or violate the rule of exclusion. An absence admits several models; choosing one requires additional evidence.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Nor does the model predict how a particular civilization would behave. A region can expand through autonomous settlements, probes or processes that we cannot imagine. Their communities could diverge and leave gaps. The smooth border serves to investigate geometry and selection; turning it into a coordinated fleet adds a narrative that equations do not contain.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The final animation is intellectually powerful because it places our question within a universe with history. As the stains grow, the point from which we look ceases to appear randomly chosen. However, by stopping the image and returning to the real sky, we do not see confirmed borders nor do we know the population that would produce them. What has been learned is more precise: under certain assumptions, our early appearance and the absence of visible regions can be analyzed together. The answer remains a contrastable hypothesis, not a countdown to advertised visitors.`}],sources:[{title:`If Loud Aliens Explain Human Earliness, Quiet Aliens Are Also Rare`,publisher:`Hanson, Martin, McCarter y Paulson, 2021`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2102.01522`},{title:`The Fermi Paradox and the Aurora Effect: Exo-civilization Settlement, Expansion and Steady States`,publisher:`Carroll-Nellenback, Frank, Wright y Scharf, 2019`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.04450`}],readingMinutes:5},{id:`civilizaciones-silenciosas`,title:`Existing without being easy to find`,lead:`A civilization can be undetectable without having decided to hide. The observed silence also belongs to our instruments.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Imagine a community that exchanges information within its own system and barely directs energy outwards. For its inhabitants there would be continuous activity. For a distant telescope, it might not stand out from the background.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Detectability and existence are different properties. The direction of emissions, their potency, their duration and our sensitivity determine what comes to be recognized. The breadth of the SETI search space forces that difference to be maintained. [1]`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Silence can be a daily consequence`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Efficient technology does not have to emit easily intercepted signals in all directions. Nor should any society devote resources to advertising. These are possibilities, not data on a known alien population.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`There are more specific proposals, such as waiting for cold times to perform computation. The summering hypothesis raised that argument, and it received criticism of its thermodynamic balance. Presenting both sides allows us to see a scientific idea under discussion, without turning it into an established explanation of silence. [2][3]`},{kind:`heading`,text:`A conversation can escape through a narrow beam`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A mature infrastructure may depend on cables, fibers or directed links instead of broadcasting. A narrow beam concentrates energy on the receiver and reduces what escapes to other places. From afar, detecting it requires finding itself in its path during the correct interval.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The detectability changes frequently, bandwidth and duration. A short signal can be intense and pass between observations; another may fall under the noise. Saying that a society uses radio is not enough to know if our instruments would recognize it. The SETI search space has many dimensions and only a part has been covered. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The activity can concentrate under surfaces or around stars different from the Sun. These elections can respond to protection and resources without any intention of hiding. They move the footprints to other bands and scales.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Efficiency moves heat`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`All energy activity ends up rejecting heat. The temperature of the radiator determines where its emission appears; the area and orientation modify what an observer receives. Thermodynamic analysis of Dyson structures connects useful work with residual radiation. [4]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A cold installation could emit in remote infrared and initially be confused with dust. A hot one would stand out in other bands. Looking for heat requires comparing natural stars, disks and galaxies; an anomaly opens an investigation, does not confirm inhabitants.`},{kind:`note`,title:`Little visible does not mean without heat`,paragraphs:[`Physical activity has an energy balance. Temperature, broadcast area and environment change how the waste heat is observed. Reducing detectability in a band does not amount to eliminating any physical consequences. [4]`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`If a proposal makes it possible to justify any absence by saying that society hides better, it loses the ability to prove. It is more useful to specify what kind of activity would exist and what observation might discover or limit it.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Stimulation offers a precise example. It proposes to reserve resources for computation in a colder future universe; a later critique discusses whether to expect delivery of the claimed thermodynamic advantage. [2][3] Exchange turns an intuition about silence into examineable assumptions.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`There are less extreme options: periods of low activity, internal communications or little visible infrastructure. Their emissions can vary for millennia while we observe an instant. “Silent” describes a relationship between source and instrument, not a personality.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`To distinguish scenarios you have to specify an energy flow, atmosphere, luminous pattern or transmission and calculate if the telescope could see it. A negative result restricts that particular case, not every technology possible.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The imagined daily life can be intense and even produce little escape. Trains are coordinated by closed networks, observatories store data locally and energy beams are activated only when receiver and emitter align. A distant telescope could lose all those moments. Instead, a large radiator would remain visible for longer. The duration of each footprint decides which search has the greatest opportunities.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`This allows for a scientific program: combining radio campaigns, atmospheric spectra, infrared and light curves, each with known sensitivity. Searches complement each other because no isolated absence describes the entire system activity.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Silence, so defined, always includes a date and an instrument.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The scene can be quiet: cities or processes that work without a lighthouse dedicated to us. Thinking about them helps to give up the idea that the universe would have to be presented in formats we immediately recognize. It also retains a necessary doubt: perhaps those places are not there. The search is to learn how to distinguish both possibilities.`}],sources:[{title:`How Much SETI Has Been Done? Finding Needles in the n-Dimensional Cosmic Haystack`,publisher:`J. T. Wright, S. Kanodia y E. Lubar, 2018`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07252`},{title:`That is not dead which can eternal lie: the aestivation hypothesis for resolving Fermi’s paradox`,publisher:`Sandberg, Armstrong y Ćirković, 2017`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.03394`},{title:`Comment on The aestivation hypothesis for resolving Fermi’s paradox`,publisher:`Charles H. Bennett, Robin Hanson y C. Jess Riedel, 2019`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.06730`},{title:`Application of the Thermodynamics of Radiation to Dyson Spheres as Work Extractors and Computational Engines`,publisher:`Jason T. Wright, 2023`,url:`https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06564`}],readingMinutes:4},{id:`berserker`,title:`A machine that continues a war without its creators`,lead:`Berserkers come from Fred Saberhagen's fiction. They allow to explore the fear of autonomous systems that survive the human purpose that originated them.`,blocks:[{kind:`paragraph`,text:`An ancient ship continues to act when the society that built it no longer exists. In Saberhagen's fiction, Berserkers are destructive machines that pursue life. It is a literary premise; there is no astronomical evidence to make it a real population. [1]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`His restlessness is born of duration. An order can continue to be executed when the circumstances that made sense of it disappeared. The machine does not need hatred to produce hostile consequences: the narrative imagines persistent objectives and powerful means.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Autonomy and purpose are separate questions`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The self-replicating systems studied for space treat production and growth. That capacity does not per se imply aggression. Converting an autonomous factory into a berserker adds a destructive target that is not deduced from engineering. [2]`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The distinction matters because fiction can confuse narrative ease with technical inevitability. A real machine also depends on resources, energy, repair and knowledge about its environment. The advanced adjective does not eliminate these dependencies.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`As an imaginative experiment, the concept invites you to ask how autonomous action is limited, how objectives are reviewed and how failure is prevented from multiplying. These are general design questions; history does not prove that there is a single solution or mandatory outcome.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Three properties that fiction brings together`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Autonomy means acting without immediate instructions. Replication means manufacturing descendants who repeat the process. Hostility means pursuing a harmful target. An explorer can be autonomous without replicating; a factory can replicate without attacking; a weapon can be hostile and dependent on supplies. The berserker combines all three, but none of them involves the others.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`To persist, it would require energy, navigation and repair. To multiply it would have to prospect, refine materials, manufacture components and test descendants. NASA's study shows the extent of that industrial chain. [2] Fiction can omit it; a technical analysis must retain it.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The destructive target requires criteria. How do you recognize life, distinguish machine organism or decide that a signal is a target? Imperfect sensors would produce false positives. An adaptable system can move away from the order; a rigid one fails in new environments.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`An order ages during the journey`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Between departure and arrival centuries pass. Creators may disappear, agree or discover that their premise was false. The machine doesn't consult on time. Their narrative danger is to faithfully execute a decision that no one can defend anymore.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Replicating amplifies faults. If each descendant inherits a defective sorter, the error extends. Testing, resource limits, version signatures and suspension can reduce risks. Designing absolute resistance to deactivation eliminates those correction pathways.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Nor would the branches be identical or synchronized. Some would lose resources, suffer breakdowns, or change. On a galactic scale they would form an industrial genealogy without common command in real time.`},{kind:`note`,title:`An absence does not identify the person responsible`,paragraphs:[`Not finding life in a place is not evidence of machines that destroyed it. To sustain this hypothesis would require positive signals and contrasted alternatives. Adding an invisible agent to each silence produces a difficult story to refute, not a confirmed explanation.`]},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A search could define specific remains: artificial materials, impacts, propulsion emissions or active machines. Each candidate would have natural and instrumental alternatives. A sterile environment does not identify a destroyer alone.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The idea does not automatically solve Fermi's paradox. Machines would have to appear, spread, detect almost every technological life and remain invisible to us. That sum of assumptions needs independent evidence.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The nearby lesson belongs to the design of long-term systems. Revisable targets, limits, logs and shutdown allow you to correct a tool when the initial reason disappears. The greater their autonomy, the more important it is to retain that possibility.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`A ship that finds an ambiguous signal offers the decisive moment. You can classify it as a target, ask for confirmation that it will take centuries or keep distance and collect more data. A literary Berserker shoots because his goal closes the doubt in advance. Responsible engineering makes that uncertainty a reason to stop. There capacity and purpose are separated: the engine allows to arrive, but the decision architecture determines what happens when doing it.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`Fiction makes that choice unforgettable precisely because it shows its absence.`},{kind:`paragraph`,text:`The scene of a machine traveling alone during ages has strength because it separates capacity and care. It can be read as a literary warning about what we decided to perpetuate. Their place in this atlas is to open up that reflection without presenting fictional monsters as a fact of the sky.`}],sources:[{title:`Saberhagen’s Worlds of SF and Fantasy`,publisher:`Sitio de la obra de Fred Saberhagen; ficción`,url:`https://berserker.com/`},{title:`Self-replicating systems: A systems engineering approach`,publisher:`NASA, 1980`,url:`https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/19800025701`}],readingMinutes:4}];export{e as default};